course: dictionary
lesson: 1623
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word chest can mean a part of your body, a large box, or a piece of furniture. To work out which one an author means, read the words around it: they are the context. «Picture the last lap on sports day: face red, chest heaving, heart thumping in your ears.» Your chest is the front of your body, because it heaves, rising and falling as you puff for breath.
explain: «Twelve pairs of ribs curve round from the spine like a cage, guarding the heart and lungs.» Here chest is the part of your body that holds your heart and lungs. Now think about pirates, who bury treasure chests. The clues are treasure and bury, not your body, so chest means a large strong box with a lid.
explain: Ask yourself: which meaning fits the sentence? Then try it in place of the word. Does "pirates bury a body part" make sense? No, so the box meaning is right.
explain: Some phrases do not mean what their words say. «Get it off your chest» means tell someone what is worrying you. Nobody is lifting anything off their body. When a phrase does not make sense word by word, ask what the whole phrase is saying.
fact: Chest can mean a part of your body, a large box with a lid, or a piece of furniture.
fact: Your chest is the front of your body, between your neck and your tummy.
fact: A chest is a large strong box with a lid, used for keeping things in, such as a toy chest.
fact: A chest of drawers is a piece of furniture with drawers for clothes.
fact: Your chest holds your heart and lungs, protected by your ribcage, and it rises and falls when you breathe.
exercise: «Her chest heaved after the race.» What does chest mean here? | A piece of furniture with drawers | *The front of your body, between your neck and your tummy | The top of a hill | A large strong box with a lid
exercise: «The toy chest was full of model cars.» What is a toy chest? | A tent for camping | A part of your body | *A large strong box with a lid for keeping toys in | A chest of drawers for clothes
exercise: Mum says, «Get it off your chest.» What does this mean? | Take off your jumper | Lift a heavy box off the floor | Stand up straight and proud | *Tell someone what is worrying you
test: «A chest of drawers should be fixed to the wall so it can't tip over.» What is a chest of drawers? | A bird's nest | *A piece of furniture with drawers for clothes | A part of your body | A large box for treasure
test: «Chest pains or a bad chest cough need a doctor.» Which meaning of chest fits here? | A chest of drawers | *The front of your body, between your neck and your tummy | A large strong box with a lid | A treasure chest
exam: Which sentence uses chest to mean a large box? | «Get it off your chest, Sam.» | «Her chest rose and fell as she breathed.» | *«He put his old comics in the chest under his bed.» | «He felt a sharp pain in his chest.»
